## 2. TREND (Imbalance / Directional)

### 2.1 Definition

> "when a market rises and takes time to do so, it usually also takes more time for the market to turn around again. That's because at the top there isn't an imbalance. It's more of a waiting situation."
> **Source GPS:** BVpYGKz9PBU | [BEGINNING]

> "markets are only about 30% truly stably trending. And about 70% they're just not."
> **Source GPS:** 43JaKHRvxHk | [BEGINNING]

**Tier:** T3 — Tom's stated statistic. Not independently verified.

### 2.2 Three Trend Structure Types

Tom distinguishes three trend structures. This is critical because each type has different implications for setup selection and target planning.

#### A. Fast / Accelerated Trend

> "Fair value gaps are price areas where the market has not made pullbacks during a fast, accelerated trend."
> **Source GPS:** 3JqMyzE8jIQ | [BEGINNING]

> "Here we had a really fast trend, very often the case that the market goes back there."
> **Source GPS:** -dMWA_bbm3o | [MIDDLE]

**Characteristics:**
- Price moves rapidly with minimal pullback.
- Creates fair value gaps (untested price areas).
- Volume may be concentrated at the beginning; thin in the middle of the move.
- High probability of return to fill gaps, but timing is uncertain.

#### B. Normal Trend

> "And that's why we distinguish between fast trends and normal trends. And yes, there are also slow creeping trends. Three different types of trend structures."
> **Source GPS:** 3JqMyzE8jIQ | [BEGINNING]

**Characteristics:**
- Steady directional move with periodic pullbacks.
- VWAP slopes in the direction of the trend.
- Price stays above VWAP (uptrend) or below VWAP (downtrend).

> "many institutional traders look at the VWAP. They say, is that the VWAP and is the price currently above it? Then they're more bullishly inclined. If the price is below it, they're initially more bearishly inclined."
> **Source GPS:** 43JaKHRvxHk | [BEGINNING]

#### C. Slow Creeping Trend

> "there are also slow creeping trends. Three different types of trend structures."
> **Source GPS:** 3JqMyzE8jIQ | [BEGINNING]

**Characteristics:**
- Gradual, persistent move with small candles.
- Often hard to distinguish from balance on short timeframes.
- Can suddenly accelerate or reverse.

**[GAP]** Tom does not provide exact quantitative thresholds (e.g., ATR, slope angle, bar count) to distinguish fast vs normal vs slow creeping trends. Classification is visual/experiential.

### 2.3 Identification Criteria

**A. Visual / Structural**
- Sustained directional move with higher highs (uptrend) or lower lows (downtrend).
- Price stays on one side of VWAP for extended periods.

> "if it's not the case that the market is trending right now, then you have situations like this here... Where you can simply see that the market is permanently above the VWAP, below the VWAP, above the VWAP"
> **Source GPS:** 43JaKHRvxHk | [BEGINNING]

**B. Volume Profile Shape**
- D profile (one-sided) or P profile = directional imbalance.
- Volume concentrated at one end of the range.

**C. VWAP Slope**
- VWAP slopes clearly up or down.
- Steeper slope = stronger trend.

> "If we want to use the volume weighted average price or VWAP as a counter trader, we can look at whether the VWAP is very steep, for example"
> **Source GPS:** 43JaKHRvxHk | [MIDDLE]

**D. Weekly Profile Trend**
- VPOC trending higher/lower across weeks = global money flow direction.

> "try drawing a trend based only on the V POX and the value areas and you'll find that you can immediately see if a market is flattening out in terms of global money flow or not."
> **Source GPS:** Ld1dhjGdTCA | [MIDDLE]

### 2.4 Valid Setups in Trend

| Setup Type | Why Valid | Source |
|-----------|-----------|--------|
| Trend-Continuation | Primary strategy. Wait for pullback to VWAP/structural level. | 43JaKHRvxHk |
| Breakout | If trend accelerates, breakout setups valid. | 3JqMyzE8jIQ |
| Counter-Trade | ONLY at extreme extensions with proof of exhaustion. High risk. | BVpYGKz9PBU, zQTbdEweG3c |

> "You should then definitely only look for a trend continuation setup here."
> **Source GPS:** 43JaKHRvxHk | [MIDDLE]

> "first rule, very important, the VWAP must be based on a trend. Only then can I see if I might enter my trade based on counter trades on this VWAP."
> **Source GPS:** 43JaKHRvxHk | [BEGINNING]

**Tier:** T5 — Tom's command.

### 2.5 Trend Health Assessment

Not all trends are equal. Tom assesses whether a trend is "healthy" or not.

**Healthy Trend:**
- Liquidity present at key levels.
- Volume supports the move.
- Pullbacks are shallow and find buyers/sellers.

**Unhealthy Trend:**
- No liquidity at the extreme (thin volume).
- Gap above/below prior structure without acceptance.
- Correlations diverging (e.g., DAX stops rising while BTC/SPX recover).

> "The idea was that this trend isn't entirely healthy. Why? Because there was simply no liquidity left here and the liquidity was down here. That means low volume."
> **Source GPS:** zQTbdEweG3c | [END]

> "the DAX future, and this is the truly decisive point, stopped rising even though it had been coming from a strong downtrend... Bitcoin... began to recover... However, the DAX future... stopped rising"
> **Source GPS:** zQTbdEweG3c | [MIDDLE]

