## 3. TRANSITION STATES

Tom recognizes that markets do not switch instantly between balance and trend. There are transitional phases.

### 3.1 Balance After Strong Trend (Sorting Itself Out)

> "when such a balance profile comes after a strong trend, then it means the market is starting to sort itself out. That doesn't mean it's going short yet. It can just as easily continue long."
> **Source GPS:** zQTbdEweG3c | [MIDDLE]

**Implication:** A balance profile after a trend is NOT a counter-trade signal by itself. It is a "wait and see" state. Need proof of direction before committing.

### 3.2 Double Distribution

> "The balance, despite swinging down sharply at times, is situated quite high up across all the weekly candles. And then we have a beautiful, what's called a double distribution here."
> **Source GPS:** -dMWA_bbm3o | [MIDDLE]

**Implication:** Two distinct balance areas separated by a thin zone. Price may oscillate between them or break through the thin zone for a fast move.

### 3.3 Market Opening Override

The opening range can override the pre-market state assessment.

> "if you put that into context and you're planning a balanced day, for example, but you see at the market opening everything is different, your result can be many times better because you know exactly now I'd better stay out of the trading day."
> **Source GPS:** Ld1dhjGdTCA | [MIDDLE] — Point 9

> "Is the market opening range very large? Or is it very small? And think about it, if something is very narrow, but liquidity is the same as in the previous days, then the probability at a later point in time is much greater that the market will trend."
> **Source GPS:** Ld1dhjGdTCA | [MIDDLE]

**Rule:**
- Narrow opening range + normal liquidity = higher probability of trend later.
- Large opening range = may indicate volatile day; reassess state.

---

## 4. STATE CLASSIFICATION WORKFLOW

Based on Tom's 13-step framework (Ld1dhjGdTCA), the state assessment is Point 3 — before any setup is considered.

### Step 1: Quick Watch (Line Chart)
> "The first thing I generally do is I give myself what I always call a quick watch of the current price trend. And for that, even a line chart would be enough... are we currently more in balance or are we more in a trend right now?"
> **Source GPS:** 3o1VVsfBmmU | [BEGINNING]

### Step 2: Weekly Profile (Macro Context)
> "The first thing we always do is look at the trend structure and the major trend structure is this one which you can see very clearly."
> **Source GPS:** BVpYGKz9PBU | [BEGINNING]

### Step 3: Daily Profile (Session Context)
> "I simply look at a period of time that is currently important to me. Where are we in balance or are we currently in an active aggressive trend?"
> **Source GPS:** Ld1dhjGdTCA | [MIDDLE]

### Step 4: VWAP Slope (Intraday Confirmation)
- Sideways VWAP = balance.
- Sloped VWAP = trend (direction = slope direction).
- Steep VWAP = fast trend.

### Step 5: Market Opening (Override Check)
- Compare opening range to plan. If different, reassess.

---

## 5. STATE → SETUP MATRIX

| Market State | Primary Setup | Secondary Setup | Avoid |
|-------------|---------------|-----------------|-------|
| Balance | Counter-Trade | Scalp | Trend-Continuation |
| Trend (healthy) | Trend-Continuation | Breakout | Counter-Trade (without proof) |
| Trend (unhealthy/extreme) | Counter-Trade (with proof) | — | Trend-Continuation |
| Transition (sorting out) | WAIT — no setup until proof | — | All |

> "Whether it's trend or balance because I have to use different setups there and that is absolutely crucial."
> **Source GPS:** Ld1dhjGdTCA | [MIDDLE]

> "you absolutely have to divide your setups that are suitable for short trades into counter trend continuation or breakout. And this is exactly where the wheat is separated from the chaff."
> **Source GPS:** BVpYGKz9PBU | [MIDDLE]

